\id BAK
\h Paschalion
\toc1 Paschalion
\toc2 Paschalion
\toc3 Paschalion
\mt1 Paschalion
\mt2 For the Years {paschalion years}
\p Pascha, on which all the movable feasts and holy days depend, is the first Sunday after the full moon that falls on or next after March 21; if that full moon falls on a Sunday, Pascha is the Sunday following. The full moon so reckoned is not the astronomical full moon but the fourteenth day of the lunar month as the ancient ecclesiastical computation reckons it, by a cycle of nineteen years. The seventh Apostolic Canon forbids keeping Pascha with the Jews before the vernal equinox, which the Byzantine canonists read as forbidding Pascha to precede or coincide with the Jewish Passover. Pascha falls no earlier than March 22 and no later than April 25, Old Style.
\p The Western Church follows the same rule, but since the Gregorian reform of 1582 it has reckoned March 21 by the Gregorian calendar and the moon by revised tables, while the Orthodox Church keeps the Julian calendar and the ancient computation, so that the two feasts often fall on different days. The Julian calendar has stood thirteen days behind the Gregorian since March 1, 1900, Old Style, and from March 1, 2100, Old Style, will stand fourteen days behind it. The table gives each date in both styles and marks with a dagger (†) the years in which Pascha and the Western Easter fall on the same day.
{paschalion}
